# Ars Magica 5th Edition Definitive - The Order of Hermes

**Version:** 0.1
**Source:** Ars Magica 5th Edition Definitive (Atlas Games, 2024)

---

## The Order of Hermes

The Order of Hermes is the preeminent magical society of Mythic Europe, a vast organization of wizards whose roots stretch back to the 8th century. It was founded through the combined efforts of **Bonisagus**, who invented the Hermetic theory of magic, and his student **Trianoma**, who persuaded twelve powerful magi from across Europe to unite under a common set of laws. These twelve magi became the Founders, and each established a House within the Order that carries on their magical traditions to this day.

The Order exists for several fundamental purposes: to protect magi from mundane interference, to prevent magical conflicts from destroying the wizards themselves, and to advance the understanding of Hermetic magic. All members are bound by the **Code of Hermes**, the oath every magus swears upon completing their apprenticeship and passing the Gauntlet. The Order spans all of Mythic Europe, from the cold reaches of Scandinavia to the shores of the Holy Land, organized into regional **Tribunals** that govern local affairs.

Within the Order, magi live in **covenants** -- shared communities where they study, conduct laboratory research, and collaborate on magical projects. A typical covenant consists of roughly half a dozen magi along with their mundane staff, though they range in size from pairs of magi to large organizations with over two dozen members and hundreds of servants. Covenants are usually situated in remote locations where **Magic auras** are strongest, often taking the form of towers, castles, or other fortified dwellings. The covenant is as much a character in an Ars Magica saga as any individual magus -- ensuring its prosperity is central to the game.

The Order maintains internal peace through a system of laws, judges, and assemblies, while collectively defending its members from external threats, whether those are hostile mundane rulers, rival supernatural powers, or rogue wizards who have broken the Oath. Two shared magical practices bind all members together: the **Parma Magica**, a personal magical shield that also serves as the Order's identifying credential (since only sworn members are taught it), and the common system of **Hermetic magic** through which all members can share knowledge, texts, and techniques regardless of their House.

### Historical Context

The Order was founded in 767 AD, taking its dating system from that year (the "ninth hundred and fifth year of Aries" as stated in the Oath). Before the Order's founding, wizards across Europe lived in isolation or small, often hostile groups, constantly warring with one another and vulnerable to persecution by mundane authorities and the Church. The **Cult of Mercury**, a magical tradition dating to the Roman Empire, had once provided some structure, but it collapsed along with Roman civilization.

Bonisagus's great breakthrough was creating a unified magical theory that could integrate the diverse magical traditions of Europe into a single, teachable system. Trianoma's genius was recognizing that this theory could serve as the foundation for a political alliance among wizards. Together, they convinced twelve of the most powerful magi in Europe to join, each bringing their own traditions, students, and knowledge into the new Order.

The Order's early centuries were not peaceful. The greatest crisis came during the **Schism War** of the early eleventh century, when House Diedne was destroyed and its members hunted down and killed. The exact causes and nature of the Schism War remain hotly debated among Hermetic historians, but its aftermath shaped the modern Order profoundly, hardening attitudes toward perceived threats and reinforcing the power of the Quaesitores.

### Structure of the Order

The Order is divided into **twelve Houses**, each reflecting the traditions, philosophies, and magical specialties of its Founder. These Houses fall into three distinct categories based on how membership is determined and what unites the members.

---

## The Three Types of Houses

### True Lineages

The four True Lineages -- **Bonisagus, Guernicus, Mercere, and Tremere** -- define membership through an unbroken chain of master-apprentice relationships stretching back to the original Founder. Every member of a True Lineage was trained by someone who was trained by someone (and so on) who was taught by the Founder personally. It is not possible to join a True Lineage after training; you must be apprenticed into one. These Houses tend to be the most stable and tradition-bound institutions within the Order.

### Mystery Cults

The four Mystery Cults -- **Bjornaer, Criamon, Merinita, and Verditius** -- are bound together by shared mystical practices and secret initiations. Membership is gained by being initiated into the House's **Outer Mystery**, a special supernatural ability unique to that House. Each Mystery Cult also guards deeper **Inner Mysteries** that are progressively revealed to worthy members. Because initiation is the gateway to membership, magi from other Houses can theoretically join a Mystery Cult, though such transfers are uncommon.

### Societates

The four Societates -- **Ex Miscellanea, Flambeau, Jerbiton, and Tytalus** -- are gatherings of magi with common interests and philosophies. Membership in these Houses is relatively fluid; magi who feel they no longer fit in their original House may transfer to a Societas that better matches their temperament. **Ex Miscellanea** is the most open of all Houses, accepting anyone with The Gift and basic competence in Hermetic fundamentals, and many of its members will actively teach prospective recruits the necessary skills.

A magus may only belong to one House at a time.

---

## House Bjornaer

**Type:** Mystery Cult
**Pronunciation:** BYORE-nayr
**Founder:** Bjornaer
**Current Prima:** Falke
**Domus Magna:** Crintera, Rhine Tribunal
**Outer Mystery:** Heartbeast

House Bjornaer is devoted to the study of animals and the animalistic side of human nature. Every member of the House possesses a **heartbeast** -- an animal form that is an expression of their innermost self. The ability to transform into one's heartbeast is the Outer Mystery of the House, granted during initiation.

The House's relationship with animals runs far deeper than mere shapeshifting. Many Bjornaer magi consider the understanding of the heartbeast and the natural world to be more important than Hermetic magic itself. This philosophy sets them apart from the rest of the Order, and has occasionally earned them the suspicion of more conventional magi. Their founder hailed from a **Germanic** rather than Roman magical tradition, which has also contributed to the House being somewhat less respected than its members might deserve.

Bjornaer magi are distinguished by a notable restriction: they **cannot bind familiars**. The concept is alien to their nature, as they already possess an animal aspect within themselves. Some Bjornaer openly mock magi who take familiars, dismissing them as seeking a substitute for a heartbeast they will never possess. Conversely, the House avoids all forms of shapeshifting other than the heartbeast, and maintains hostile relations with certain other shapeshifters for reasons that outsiders find difficult to comprehend.

The House is organized into **six clans**, each with a distinct purpose:

- **Clan Arelie** -- preserving memory and the knowledge of the past
- **Clan Ilfetu** -- conducting initiations for House members
- **Clan Maruhs** -- pursuing self-realization
- **Clan Midusulf** -- providing leadership
- **Clan Sirnas** -- protecting the House
- **Clan Wilkis** -- nurturing magi and strengthening the House

Philosophically, House Bjornaer is split between two camps. The **Wilderists** follow the original philosophy of the House, advocating for the restoration of wilderness and the retreat of human civilization back to the cities. The **Harmonists**, whose ideas trace back to a tenth-century magus named Hérisson, seek a balance between humanity and nature. The Harmonist philosophy only gained widespread acceptance after the Schism War.

The former Primus, **Archmagus Urgen**, chief of Clan Midusulf, still resides at Crintera and sits on the House council, maintaining considerable influence even after ceding formal leadership.

---

## House Bonisagus

**Type:** True Lineage
**Pronunciation:** BOH-nee-SAH-goos
**Founder:** Bonisagus (magical theory) and Trianoma (political organization)
**Current Prima:** Archmaga Murion
**Domus Magna:** Durenmar, Rhine Tribunal

House Bonisagus holds a unique and prestigious position within the Order, for it was **Bonisagus himself** who invented the Hermetic theory of magic that makes the Order possible. His student **Trianoma** was the political mastermind who convinced the other Founders to join together, creating the organizational framework of the Order. The House is a True Lineage, with every member able to trace their training lineage back to the Founder.

The House is divided into **two distinct strands**. The first follows the legacy of Bonisagus himself: **theoretical researchers** who push the boundaries of what Hermetic magic can accomplish. These magi are the undisputed masters of Magic Theory, constantly refining and expanding the system that Bonisagus created. The second strand follows Trianoma's legacy: **politicians and diplomats** (often called "magi Trianomae") who work to keep the Order united, mediating conflicts and maintaining the political fabric of Hermetic society.

The House's current leader, **Archmaga Murion**, is a consummate politician who resides at Durenmar. The House tends to remember its great researchers more readily than its politicians. Notable historical figures include **Notatus**, the first Primus, who created the Aegis of the Hearth (a foundational defensive ritual used by virtually every covenant), and **Conciatta**, who extended Hermetic magic to directly affect the other supernatural realms (Divine, Faerie, and Infernal).

Among contemporary members, **Archmagus Relisius** is well known for his Christian faith and his advocacy for harmony between the Magical and Divine realms.

House Bonisagus possesses a special legal privilege: members have the right to **claim the apprentice of any other magus** in the Order if the apprentice would be more valuable to the Bonisagus researcher's studies. This right is upheld by the Peripheral Code and is one of the few truly asymmetric privileges in the Order.

---

## House Criamon

**Type:** Mystery Cult
**Pronunciation:** KREE-ah-mone
**Founder:** Criamon
**Current Prima:** Muscaria
**Domus Magna:** Cave of Twisting Shadows, Greater Alps Tribunal
**Outer Mystery:** The Enigma (Enigmatic Wisdom)

House Criamon is among the most secretive and bewildering Houses in the Order. Its members are recognizable by the **arcane symbols** they mark on their faces and bodies, and by their often otherworldly demeanor. They have little interest in the politics of the Order, disdain mere power, and pursue a mystical path that most outsiders find impenetrable.

At the heart of House Criamon lies the pursuit of the **Enigma**, a philosophical and mystical framework that the House believes can release all souls from a form of cosmic imprisonment. Their philosophy draws inspiration from the ancient Greek thinker **Empedocles** and concerns itself deeply with the nature of time. Members develop a unique moral and ethical perspective through their studies, though magi outside the House frequently have no idea what that perspective actually is. The common stereotype of Criamon magi among other wizards is that they are tattooed eccentrics whose actions make no sense.

Even by the standards of the other Mystery Cults, House Criamon is exceptionally difficult for outsiders to understand. The House's current Prima, **Muscaria**, is notably young and intensely practical -- quite unlike the common image of her House. She resides at the domus magna, the Cave of Twisting Shadows, in the Greater Alps.

Important historical members include **Vederis**, who authored the Travels of Fedoso, an allegorical text popular both within and beyond the House, and **Abdkypris**, who discovered significant connections between the House's Mysteries and the traditions of Sufi Islam. **St. Nerius**, a magus renowned for his Christian devotion and sanctity, is generally believed to have been a member of House Criamon.

---

## House Ex Miscellanea

**Type:** Societas
**Pronunciation:** EKS mis-kel-LAH-nay-ah
**Founder:** Pralix (originally a rival to the Order)
**Current Primus:** Ebroin
**Domus Magna:** Cad Gadu, Stonehenge Tribunal

House Ex Miscellanea is the largest, most diverse, and least organized of the twelve Houses. It was originally founded by **Pralix** as a rival institution to the Order of Hermes, but was eventually accepted as a House within the Order. It accepts wizards of all kinds -- some only nominally Hermetic -- whose magic comes from a wide array of disparate traditions.

Members of the House are often dismissed as **"hedge wizards"** by detractors within the Order, though most can wield Hermetic magic just as capably as any other magus. The House's membership is bound together primarily by the fact that its members do not fit neatly anywhere else. Each member typically belongs to one of many internal **traditions**, each with its own unique strengths, weaknesses, and magical specialties.

Ex Miscellanea is the easiest House to join. It will accept anyone with The Gift who possesses a basic working knowledge of Latin, Magic Theory, and the ability to raise a Parma Magica. Many members will actively teach these fundamentals to potential recruits.

The current Primus, **Ebroin**, is a relatively young magus who was elected after the previous Prima, Immanola, was declared to have entered Final Twilight. He has ambitions to revitalize the House from its domus magna of Cad Gadu in the Stonehenge Tribunal.

One of Pralix's closest allies was **Colomen**, from a tradition of warding wizards now called the "Columbae." His tradition's magic was shared with other magi and formed the basis for most of the warding spells used in Hermetic magic. Beyond this contribution, most magi would struggle to name any famous member of Ex Miscellanea they had not met personally.

---

## House Flambeau

**Type:** Societas
**Pronunciation:** flahm-BOH
**Founder:** Flambeau
**Current Primus:** Garus
**Domus Magna:** Castra Solis, Provencal Tribunal

House Flambeau is the martial arm of the Order. Its members specialize in **fire magic and destructive spells**, and they are known for their fearlessness, aggression, and love of combat. While they frequently cause trouble within the Order and sometimes anger mundanes through their recklessness, their willingness to fight makes them invaluable when the Order requires military force.

The House honors two traditions of destructive magic. **Flambeau the Founder** was a master of fire, specializing in the Hermetic Art of Ignem. His first apprentice and first Primus, **Apromor**, favored the Art of Perdo (destruction without the need for fire). Both traditions are respected within the House, giving members a choice between flashy conflagration and efficient annihilation.

Beyond raw destructive power, there is a strong tradition of **honor and chivalry** within House Flambeau. Under the current Primus, **Garus**, this knightly aspect has been emphasized, making the House function in many ways as the Order's warrior elite. Garus leads from Castra Solis in the Provencal Tribunal. The House's original domus magna was **Val Negra**, also in the Provencal Tribunal, but it has been lost within a regio (a pocket dimension) for decades or possibly centuries.

Flambeau's second apprentice, **Elaine**, carried on the fire magic tradition and served as a Hoplite (a combat-specialist who supports the Order's investigators). She was also a notable scholar, authoring several important texts including the Ars Flambonis, a summa on Ignem that remains popular within the House.

Among contemporary members, **Archmaga Geirlaug** of Montverte covenant in the Normandy Tribunal is particularly notable for both her great age and great power, though she is rarely seen outside her tower.

---

## House Guernicus

**Type:** True Lineage
**Pronunciation:** GWAIR-nee-kuss
**Founder:** Guernicus
**Current Prima:** Archmaga Bilera
**Domus Magna:** Magvillus, Roman Tribunal
**Key Role:** Quaesitores (investigators and judges of the Order)

House Guernicus serves as the **judicial branch** of the Order of Hermes. Its members are the investigators, prosecutors, and legal scholars who enforce the Code of Hermes and the Peripheral Code. **Guernicus the Founder** believed that without strictly enforced rules, the Order would tear itself apart through internal conflict, and his followers maintain that conviction.

The House is sometimes informally called **House Quaesitor**, as the title "Quaesitor" denotes those magi empowered by the Order to investigate Hermetic crimes. While House Guernicus trains its own apprentices as Quaesitores, the role is not exclusive to the House. One of the highest honors in the Order is to be invited by House Guernicus's elders to become a Quaesitor, and magi who receive this honor retain membership in their original House.

Quaesitores are frequently asked to investigate suspected crimes or mediate disputes between magi or covenants. Tradition requires that those requesting Quaesitorial service offer a few pawns of vis as compensation. The investigators are often accompanied by **Hoplites** -- magi from various Houses who specialize in magical combat and can handle dangerous situations that a Quaesitor might encounter.

The House is divided by an important philosophical debate. **Traditionalists** believe the Code should be preserved exactly as it has existed since the Founding. **Transitionalists**, a movement begun by Simprim of Guernicus in the mid-twelfth century, believe the Code needs revision to better address modern realities and seek expanded legal powers for the Quaesitores.

The current Prima, **Archmaga Bilera**, has a long record of service and maintains a largely neutral stance on the Traditionalist-Transitionalist debate. She resides at Magvillus in the Roman Tribunal.

Guernicus himself was a master of **earth magic** and taught Bonisagus much of the knowledge that was incorporated into the Hermetic Art of Terram. A tradition of earth magic persists in the House, with its most famous living practitioner being **Ausculator**, leader of the covenant of Hedyosmos in the Theban Tribunal, which is rumored to possess a portal to the underworld.

---

## House Jerbiton

**Type:** Societas
**Pronunciation:** YARE-bih-tahn
**Founder:** Jerbiton
**Current Primus:** Andru
**Domus Magna:** Valnastium, Greater Alps Tribunal

House Jerbiton is the most culturally engaged House in the Order, dedicated to the **arts, scholarship, and the mundane world**. Its members serve as the Order's primary link to the nobility and the Church, and they often take it upon themselves to maintain good relations between Hermetic and mundane society.

Many Jerbiton magi come from **aristocratic backgrounds** -- the House actively recruits apprentices from the nobility, and these magi maintain their social connections after joining the Order. Others are drawn from talented artists and craftsmen who happen to possess The Gift. The Gentle Gift, which lacks the usual social penalties, is particularly common in this House, making its members some of the few magi who can interact comfortably with ordinary people.

Other Houses sometimes distrust House Jerbiton, believing its members are too closely tied to mundane powers. The Jerbiton perspective is the opposite: they fear that the Order has become dangerously isolated from humanity, risking violent conflict with the mundane world. They work energetically to bridge this gap, pursuing aesthetic excellence and Classical learning with passion.

The fall of Constantinople to the Fourth Crusade in 1204 galvanized the House. The current Primus, **Andru**, used the tragedy to rally the House back to its original values of defending civilization, a call that the younger generation has embraced.

The House is loosely organized into **leagues** based on common interests. A maga may belong to multiple leagues simultaneously. Examples include **The Gastronomers** (who cooperate to host an annual lavish party, popular enough to attract members from other Houses), **The Silent Ones** (who seek to eliminate the need for words and gestures in Hermetic magic), and **The Humble Brethren** (who work to subtly influence the College of Cardinals to prevent anti-magic sentiment from reaching the papacy).

Historically important Primi include **Petrus** (tenth century), who advocated living within mundane communities, and **Mattieus** (twelfth century), who believed Jerbiton should live among other magi and serve as ambassadors. Both philosophies remain active.

Notable historical figures include **Fortunata of Jerbiton**, an eleventh-century spirit master who gathered great knowledge and power from many Mysteries before being driven into exile and Final Twilight. Among contemporary members, **Archmagus Mathieus of Constantinople** is known for having abandoned magical study entirely, though the fall of Constantinople rekindled his sense of purpose. **Archmagus Edward of Milton** heads the covenant of Schola Pythagoranis in Cambridge, which claims to have helped nucleate the university there.

---

## House Mercere

**Type:** True Lineage
**Pronunciation:** mare-KAY-ray (Classical Latin) or mare-SARE (Vulgar Latin)
**Founder:** Mercere
**Current Prima:** Insatella (unGifted)
**Domus Magna:** Harco, Roman Tribunal
**Key Role:** Redcaps (messengers of the Order)

House Mercere is unique among the Houses because its founder **lost his magical powers** but chose to remain involved in the Order by assuming the vital non-magical role of messenger. His followers continue this tradition, and the House serves as the **communications network** of the Order of Hermes.

Members of House Mercere are more commonly known as **Redcaps**, named for the distinctive headgear they wear as a badge of office. Remarkably, all members of the House -- whether Gifted or unGifted -- are officially recognized as magi of the Order. Even unGifted Redcaps undergo the standard fifteen-year apprenticeship. Redcaps are permitted to attend Hermetic Tribunals, but by convention, only those who possess The Gift exercise voting rights.

House Mercere is a True Lineage, and its small **Gifted tradition** is composed almost entirely of blood descendants of the Founder. Mercere trained two apprentices before losing his Gift, establishing the lineage that continues to this day. Many magi of this House have pursued a strong interest in the magic of the ancient **Cult of Mercury**, a pre-Hermetic magical tradition. Some unGifted Mercere possess powerful supernatural abilities attributed to descent from the heroes of classical mythology.

The most celebrated member of the House (after the Founder) is **Belin**, Mercere's last apprentice. Despite having no Gift, she was renowned for her devotion to the Order and her tireless dedication to delivering messages, and remains a role model within the House.

The current Prima, **Insatella**, is an unGifted Redcap who inherited the role from her father. She manages the House's affairs from Harco in the Roman Tribunal. Because she lacks The Gift, she deliberately avoids Hermetic politics, reinforcing the House's long-standing reputation for neutrality.

---

## House Merinita

**Type:** Mystery Cult
**Pronunciation:** meh-rih-NEE-tah
**Founder:** Merinita (though the faerie focus was established by Quendalon)
**Current Primus:** Handri
**Domus Magna:** Irencillia, Rhine Tribunal
**Outer Mystery:** Faerie Magic

House Merinita is dedicated to the **world of faeries**, and its members tend to be as strange and otherworldly as the creatures they study. Merinita magi frequently remain isolated from the rest of the Order, involving themselves in Hermetic affairs mainly when they need to defend faeries from the assaults of other wizards. They eschew the merely mortal and seek answers in the mysterious realm of Arcadia.

The House's history is marked by an unusual succession crisis. **Merinita the Founder** was not actually interested in faeries; that focus was introduced by her first apprentice, **Quendalon**, who had been raised among faeries and believed they had much to offer. After Merinita disappeared at the end of the eighth century, Quendalon announced he was leaving to study with the faeries, placing Merinita's second apprentice, **Myanar**, in charge.

When Quendalon returned two years later -- his eyes replaced by rubies -- he declared a new direction for the House. This was resisted by Myanar, and civil war erupted within the House. Quendalon ultimately prevailed, and under his influence, the House turned fully toward the study of faerie. He served as Primus until the end of the tenth century, though he had almost no contact with his House during the latter half of that time. Some magi even now suspect that the being who returned was not truly Quendalon but a faerie wearing his form.

The current Primus, **Handri**, lives at the domus magna of Irencillia in the Rhine Tribunal. The previous Prima, **Vinaria**, also resides there. She vanished in 1202 much as Quendalon had centuries before, leaving Handri in charge. When she returned in 1209, she made no attempt to reclaim leadership and continues to live quietly at Irencillia.

**Lucian the Scholar** is an elder magus who lives in the covenant of Aegea on the floor of the Aegean Sea in the Theban Tribunal, renowned for his knowledge of Byzantine and ancient Greek literary traditions. **Dama**, the Praeco (eldest magus) of the Provencal Tribunal, was born in 993, fell into Twilight in 1061, and emerged nineteen years later. She is rumored to lead a Mystery Cult that is separate from House Merinita.

---

## House Tremere

**Type:** True Lineage
**Pronunciation:** tray-MARE-ay or tray-MARE
**Founder:** Tremere
**Current Prima:** Poena
**Domus Magna:** Coeris, Transylvanian Tribunal
**Notable Contribution:** Invented certamen (formal magical dueling)

House Tremere is the most hierarchical and disciplined of the twelve Houses. Its members emphasize **judgment, strategy, and detailed planning**, believing in respect for superiors and asserting authority over subordinates. Dignity is paramount. The House is widely considered one of the more sensible and stable institutions in the Order, providing strength and courage when needed while exercising restraint when peace better serves the Order's interests.

As a True Lineage, every member of House Tremere can trace their apprenticeship chain back to **Tremere the Founder**, who invented **certamen** -- the formal, ritualized method of magical dueling that allows magi to settle disputes without killing each other. The House retains a deep interest in certamen, and its members receive special training in the practice.

House Tremere maintains a distinctive custom regarding **voting sigils**. A Tremere parens (master) holds the voting sigil of their filius (apprentice) until the filius defeats the parens in certamen, or until the parens dies. If a magus who does not hold his own sigil trains an apprentice, that new magus's sigil is passed up the chain to the first ancestor who holds their own sigil. This results in the House's Tribunal votes being concentrated in very few hands, giving its senior members disproportionate political power.

The current Prima, **Poena**, is said to know the names, magical strengths, and preferences of every member of her House, as well as any other magus she considers potentially useful. She manages the House's projects with remarkable efficiency from Coeris in the Transylvanian Tribunal. Unusually among the Houses, Tremere has a formal **second-in-command**: **Archmaga Monica Ierne**, originally from the Stonehenge Tribunal and a master of combat magic. She has publicly acknowledged that she is unsuited to be Prima, but serves as an excellent field commander.

**Archmagus Stentorius** exemplifies the stereotype of a senior Tremere: the leader of Fengheld (the largest covenant in the Rhine Tribunal), he holds three-fifths of the Tremere sigils in that Tribunal. Even in a Tribunal that contains the domus magnae and Primi of Houses Bonisagus, Bjornaer, and Merinita, Stentorius may hold more political power than any other individual.

House Tremere is in many ways the philosophical opposite of **House Tytalus**. Where Tremere values stability and respect for elders, Tytalus values change and constant challenge. The two Founders were trained by the same wizard, **Guorna the Fetid**, and fought frequently over their contrasting visions.

---

## House Tytalus

**Type:** Societas
**Pronunciation:** TIE-tah-loos
**Founder:** Tytalus
**Current Primus/Prima:** Disputed between Buliste and Harpax
**Domus Magna:** Fudarus, Normandy Tribunal

House Tytalus is devoted to **mastering all forms of conflict**. Its members promote competition and innovation in every kind of contest, and do not feel truly alive unless they are engaged in constant struggle. They perpetually test themselves and others, probing for strengths and weaknesses, and believe that challenge and adversity are the paths to greatness.

The House carries a dark stain in its history. In the tenth century, its leadership fell prey to **demonic corruption**. The House's masters of intrigue arrogantly believed they could manipulate the forces of Hell for their own ends, but despite their considerable skill, they were unable to outwit the Infernal. The leaders were discovered and executed for the crime of diabolism, and House Tytalus has been viewed with suspicion ever since.

The House's leadership is currently **contested**. **Buliste** was the ninth Prima but passed into Twilight. **Harpax** then claimed the title of tenth Primus. When Buliste emerged from Twilight, she refused to acknowledge Harpax's authority, and he refused to yield. The two continue to fight for control of the House and its domus magna of Fudarus in the Normandy Tribunal. Most magi of the House choose sides, but many seem to do so simply for the pleasure of the contest rather than out of genuine loyalty.

---

## House Verditius

**Type:** Mystery Cult
**Pronunciation:** ware-DEE-tee-oos
**Founder:** Verditius
**Current Primus:** Stouritus
**Domus Magna:** Verdi, Roman Tribunal
**Outer Mystery:** Verditius Magic (enhanced item creation)

House Verditius possesses unsurpassed skill in **creating enchanted items**, making its members invaluable to other magi and to non-Gifted persons who seek magical devices. The House's Outer Mystery, Verditius Magic, grants its members extraordinary abilities in the crafting of magical objects.

Almost all members of the House have inherited their founder's **magical flaw**: they cannot cast Formulaic spells without the aid of casting tools. This dependence on physical implements sometimes causes other magi to view them as inferior. The Verditius themselves reject this assessment entirely, considering themselves superior to other magi -- a hubris that is, in fact, a side effect of the Inner Mysteries of their House.

While the Code of Hermes forbids magi from selling enchanted items directly to mundanes, Verditius magi maintain mundane business associates called **venditores** who handle such transactions on their behalf. The magi sell items directly to other magi, and can accumulate considerable wealth in both magical and mundane terms.

The House has a tradition of **vendettas** -- prolonged rivalries between individual magi that persist until one concedes the other's superiority. Since concessions are rare, vendettas can last for decades. The House considers such contests honorable, though outsiders may disagree. These competitive dynamics reflect the House's politically fragmented nature, in which the Primus is the only formal position of authority.

Every **eighteen years**, the House holds a grand **Contest**, a prestigious competition in which enchanted items are judged to determine the finest created since the last Contest. Every magus in the House may enter a single item, and many spend years preparing their submissions.

The Primus is chosen by **strict primogeniture** -- the eldest living filius of the previous Primus inherits the role. This system encounters almost no opposition, as most members appreciate not having to worry about leadership elections. The current Primus, **Stouritus**, does not reside at the domus magna of Verdi in the Roman Tribunal. He is a member of the covenant of Ingasia in the Theban Tribunal and would prefer it to become the new domus magna so he need not relocate.

---

## The Code of Hermes

The **Code of Hermes** (also called the **Hermetic Oath**) is the foundational legal document of the Order. Every magus recites and swears the Oath upon completing their Gauntlet and being admitted to the Order. The Code was drafted to give magi as much personal freedom as possible while protecting them from each other. It was first sworn by Bonisagus himself, and his name appears in the text whenever it is written down.

There is a slight difference between the Code sworn by members of House Bonisagus and the Code sworn by all other Houses: Bonisagus magi pledge to share their discoveries with the Order, while all others concede to Bonisagus the right to claim their apprentice if the apprentice would be valuable to Bonisagus's research.

The degree to which magi obey the Oath varies widely -- from magus to magus, covenant to covenant, and Tribunal to Tribunal. However, magi who violate the Oath and are caught typically face serious consequences.

### Key Provisions of the Code

**Preservation of Magical Power:** Magi must not deprive or attempt to deprive any member of the Order of their magical power. This clause is broadly interpreted to cover not just direct attacks on The Gift but also indirect actions such as destroying a magus's laboratory or killing their servants, since these deprive the magus of resources needed for magical advancement.

**Prohibition on Killing:** Magi must not slay or attempt to slay any member of the Order, except in a formally declared Wizards' War. This is the foundation of internal peace within the Order.

**Wizards' War:** The Code provides a legal mechanism for lethal conflict. One magus may declare Wizards' War on another by sending a formal declaration that arrives by the next full moon. The war begins at the rise of the following full moon and lasts one full lunar month. During this time, the two magi may attack each other without legal consequence. However, excessive or unjust use of Wizards' War is discouraged, and magi who declare war too often may themselves become targets of a Wizard's March.

**Abiding by Tribunal Decisions:** All magi must respect the decisions made by fair vote at Tribunal. Each magus receives one vote, to be used prudently, and must respect the equal votes of all others. This clause gives Tribunals the authority to make new rules and interpret the Code.

**Non-Interference with Mundanes:** Magi must not interfere with the affairs of mundanes in a way that brings ruin upon other magi. This is the most controversial clause in the Code, since covenants must interact with the mundane world to exist. Interpretation centers on the qualifying phrase -- as long as dealings with mundanes do not harm other magi or seem likely to cause such harm, they are generally permitted. Many precedents have established, however, that serving as a court wizard violates the Code.

**Prohibition on Dealing with Demons:** The Order hunts down and kills any member found dealing with demons. This is the only clause enforced with absolute rigor. Even attempting to destroy demons can draw criticism if it brings Infernal attention to the Order.

**Faerie Relations:** Magi must not molest the faeries lest their vengeance harm the Order. Interpretation of what constitutes "molestation" is highly contested, partly because faerie territories are valuable vis sources. Unlike the mundane and demonic clauses, this provision does not prohibit friendly dealings with faeries.

**Prohibition on Magical Scrying:** Magi must not use magic to spy on other members of the Order. Tribunals have enforced this clause with surprising strictness, ruling in various cases that scrying on a non-magus that incidentally reveals a magus's activities is illegal, that walking around invisible constitutes scrying, and that using Intellego magic on a magus even unknowingly can be punished. Contradictory rulings exist, but the general trend is toward strict interpretation.

**Training of Apprentices:** Magi who train apprentices must ensure they swear the Code, and must be the first to strike down any apprentice who turns against the Order. In practice, this clause is lightly enforced -- magi are not required to train apprentices, and the obligation to hunt down rogue apprentices is treated more as a social expectation than a legal duty.

**Notably Missing: Magical Creatures.** The Code deliberately omits any clause protecting magical creatures or non-Hermetic wizards. This was intentional, as Trianoma wanted the Order to retain the ability to compel others to join. Excessive interference with powerful magical beings can, however, fall under the general prohibition on endangering the Order.

---

## The Peripheral Code

The **Peripheral Code** consists of every decision made at Tribunal since the founding of the Order. Its authority derives from the Code's requirement that magi abide by Tribunal decisions, but since every case is different, later Tribunals are not bound by the rulings of earlier ones. Contradictory precedents are common throughout the Peripheral Code, and few magi outside the Quaesitores are familiar with all its details.

In theory, the only penalty for breaking the Code is **death**. In practice, Tribunals rarely impose the ultimate sanction, preferring lesser punishments such as vis fines, periods of service, or other penalties. These lesser punishments are backed by the threat of the ultimate sanction: refusing to submit to a Tribunal's judgment is always treated as grounds for the death penalty.

### Wizard's March

When a magus is condemned to death, the Tribunal issues a **Wizard's March**, formally casting the offender out of the Order and stripping them of the Code's protection. A magus subject to a March is called a **"renounced magus."** By tradition, the criminal's possessions are granted to whoever kills them, which ensures that powerful magi are often eager to participate in the hunt.

---

## Tribunals

**Tribunals** are the governing assemblies of the Order, central to its political life. Originally, a Tribunal was simply any formal gathering of magi that made decisions by majority vote and recorded those decisions. Over time, the Peripheral Code provided more structure, and the modern Tribunal system consists of the Grand Tribunal and the regional Tribunals formally established by it.

### Requirements for a Legal Tribunal

A valid Tribunal must be attended in person by at least **twelve magi** from at least **four different covenants**. One **Quaesitor** must be present to oversee the legality of the proceedings; while the Quaesitor does not vote, they count toward the quorum. Magi may vote by proxy, giving their voting sigils to someone attending on their behalf, with whatever conditions the granting magus specifies.

The Tribunal is chaired by the **Praeco**, the oldest magus present. The Praeco cannot vote except to break ties, but has the power to set the order of business, and in extreme circumstances may silence or eject a magus. If ejections render the Tribunal inquorate or remove the presiding Quaesitor, the Tribunal becomes invalid. At the end of proceedings, the presiding Quaesitor must certify the Tribunal as valid -- serving as the primary check on the Praeco's power.

### The Grand Tribunal

The most important Tribunal in the Order is the **Grand Tribunal**, held **every 33 years** at **Durenmar**, the domus magna of House Bonisagus and the birthplace of the Order. Three representatives from each regional Tribunal attend, along with the Primi of all twelve Houses. The Primus of House Bonisagus serves as Praeco (regardless of whether older magi are present), and the Primus of House Guernicus serves as Presiding Quaesitor.

The Grand Tribunal addresses matters affecting the entire Order, and its decisions carry far more weight than those of regional Tribunals. It is the only Tribunal with authority over the whole Order, and therefore the only forum where inter-Tribunal disputes can be resolved.

### Regional Tribunals

Each regional Tribunal encompasses all the magi living in a defined area of Mythic Europe. These boundaries are roughly fixed by Grand Tribunal decisions, but the magi within each region may change their Tribunal's name and set membership requirements freely. Regional Tribunals meet **every seven years**, and the Redcaps are required to ensure that every magus in the region receives an invitation. Otherwise, they follow the standard rules for all Tribunals.

### The Thirteen Tribunals of the Order

The Order of Hermes in 1220 is divided into thirteen regional Tribunals:

1. **The Rhine Tribunal** -- Covers the heartland of the Holy Roman Empire along the Rhine. Home to Durenmar (domus magna of Bonisagus), Crintera (Bjornaer), and Irencillia (Merinita). The oldest and most politically complex Tribunal.

2. **The Normandy Tribunal** -- Covers the Kingdom of France. Home to Fudarus (domus magna of Tytalus). Known for its elaborate system of Hermetic tournaments.

3. **The Provencal Tribunal** -- Covers southern France. Home to Castra Solis (domus magna of Flambeau). The region where the Founder Flambeau was most active.

4. **The Roman Tribunal** -- Covers the Italian Peninsula. Home to Magvillus (domus magna of Guernicus), Harco (Mercere), and Verdi (Verditius). The most densely populated Tribunal in terms of magi.

5. **The Greater Alps Tribunal** -- Covers the Alpine regions. Home to Valnastium (domus magna of Jerbiton) and the Cave of Twisting Shadows (Criamon). A small but prestigious Tribunal.

6. **The Theban Tribunal** -- Covers Greece and the Aegean. Operates under unique democratic traditions distinct from the rest of the Order.

7. **The Transylvanian Tribunal** -- Covers the lands east of the Holy Roman Empire. Home to Coeris (domus magna of Tremere). The most tightly organized Tribunal, strongly influenced by House Tremere.

8. **The Iberian Tribunal** -- Covers the Iberian Peninsula (modern Spain and Portugal). A region shaped by the ongoing Reconquista and the interplay between Christian and Islamic cultures.

9. **The Stonehenge Tribunal** -- Covers England and Wales. Home to Cad Gadu (domus magna of Ex Miscellanea). A Tribunal with a strong tradition of independence.

10. **The Hibernian Tribunal** -- Covers Ireland. Known for its unusual local customs and the strong influence of native Irish magical traditions.

11. **The Loch Leglean Tribunal** -- Covers Scotland. One of the smaller and more remote Tribunals.

12. **The Novgorod Tribunal** -- Covers the vast lands of Rus and Scandinavia. The largest Tribunal by area but one of the most sparsely populated.

13. **The Levant Tribunal** -- Covers the Holy Land and the eastern Mediterranean. A Tribunal deeply affected by the Crusades and the clash of cultures and faiths.

---

## Key Roles within the Order

### Quaesitores

The **Quaesitores** (singular: Quaesitor) are the investigators and judges of the Order. Most are members of House Guernicus, though distinguished magi from other Houses may be invited to join their ranks. They investigate suspected violations of the Code, mediate disputes between magi and covenants, and prosecute cases at Tribunal. A Quaesitor's role is akin to that of a combined detective, prosecutor, and magistrate. Their authority derives from House Guernicus's founding commitment to enforcing the rule of law within the Order.

At Tribunals, the presiding Quaesitor serves a vital constitutional function: they oversee the legality of proceedings, ensure the quorum is maintained, and must certify the Tribunal as valid at its conclusion. Without a presiding Quaesitor, no Tribunal can function.

### Hoplites

**Hoplites** are magi who specialize in magical combat and serve as the enforcement arm of the Order's justice system. They typically accompany Quaesitores on dangerous investigations, providing martial support against threats that an investigator might not be equipped to handle alone. Hoplites may come from any House, though members of House Flambeau and House Tremere are particularly common in this role. The term is also used more broadly for any magus who serves the Order in a martial capacity, such as those who participate in Wizard's Marches against renounced magi.

### Redcaps

**Redcaps** are the messengers and postal service of the Order, predominantly members of House Mercere. They carry letters, news, vis shipments, and other communications between covenants across all of Mythic Europe. Most Redcaps are unGifted, relying on mundane travel skills, supernatural abilities inherited from their lineage, or magical items rather than Hermetic spells. Despite lacking magical power, they are afforded the full legal protections of the Code as recognized members of the Order.

Redcaps also play a crucial administrative role: they are responsible for ensuring every magus in a Tribunal receives notice of upcoming Tribunal meetings, and they maintain the information networks that keep the far-flung Order connected. A covenant that mistreats a Redcap may find itself cut off from news and deliveries -- an isolation that can be devastating in the interconnected world of Hermetic politics.

### Praeco

The **Praeco** of a Tribunal is the oldest magus present at a Tribunal meeting. This elder serves as chair of the proceedings, controlling the order of business and maintaining order. The Praeco cannot vote except to break ties, but wields considerable procedural power. At the Grand Tribunal, the Primus of House Bonisagus serves as Praeco by tradition, regardless of whether older magi are in attendance.

### Primus/Prima

Each House is led by a **Primus** (masculine) or **Prima** (feminine). The method of selecting the House leader varies: some Houses hold elections, others follow primogeniture, and still others use unique traditions. The Primus or Prima resides at the House's domus magna (though exceptions exist, as with Stouritus of House Verditius), represents the House at the Grand Tribunal, and sets the direction and policy of their House.

---

## Summary of the Twelve Houses

| House | Type | Focus | Domus Magna | Leader |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Bjornaer | Mystery Cult | Heartbeast, animals, nature | Crintera (Rhine) | Falke |
| Bonisagus | True Lineage | Magic theory and Order politics | Durenmar (Rhine) | Murion |
| Criamon | Mystery Cult | The Enigma, mystical philosophy | Cave of Twisting Shadows (Greater Alps) | Muscaria |
| Ex Miscellanea | Societas | Diverse magical traditions | Cad Gadu (Stonehenge) | Ebroin |
| Flambeau | Societas | Fire magic, destruction, combat | Castra Solis (Provencal) | Garus |
| Guernicus | True Lineage | Law, investigation, justice | Magvillus (Roman) | Bilera |
| Jerbiton | Societas | Arts, scholarship, mundane relations | Valnastium (Greater Alps) | Andru |
| Mercere | True Lineage | Messaging, communication | Harco (Roman) | Insatella |
| Merinita | Mystery Cult | Faeries, Arcadia | Irencillia (Rhine) | Handri |
| Tremere | True Lineage | Strategy, hierarchy, certamen | Coeris (Transylvanian) | Poena |
| Tytalus | Societas | Conflict, competition, challenge | Fudarus (Normandy) | Disputed |
| Verditius | Mystery Cult | Enchanted item creation | Verdi (Roman) | Stouritus |
